1: # Course frame: Health, grades 6 to 8
2: 
3: Course `health-grades-6-to-8` (catalog id `health-6-8`), grades 6, 7 and 8, one band course with one part per grade year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 30 through the band: 10 in grade 6 (units 1 to 10), 11 in grade 7 (units 11 to 21), 9 in grade 8
8:   (units 22 to 30). Eight of them are sensitive units (9, 10, 19, 20, 21, 28, 29, 30), each removable. Each year is
9:   whole on its own: its own units, end-of-unit assessments, mid-year check and year-end check.
10: - **Days.** 144: 48 a year for three years. Each year has 44 days in units and 4 course-level days (a mid-year check
11:   and its respond day, a year-end check and its respond day). One or two lessons a week, three every two weeks,
12:   each about 45 minutes, over the whole 32-week school year, so the 45 minutes hold the opening scenario (five
13:   minutes at most), the work, collecting sheets and putting cards away. The dose is the health approach page's
14:   default (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, Pacing: 6 to 8 about 45 sessions a year of 45 minutes, kept by Cory
15:   2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-2). This frame sets 48, three over "about 45", for one reason: three lessons every
16:   two weeks is a rhythm a middle school can timetable for the whole year (an A week of two and a B week of one), and
17:   it puts the mid-year check on course day 25 in week 17 and the year-end check in week 32, both off every core
18:   check day. Days are weighed by load, not equal shares: a row that is one plain fact shares a day with its
19:   neighbor, a row that teaches or returns a step card gets a model day and a pair-practice day, a unit that ends in
20:   a product (an advocacy piece, a team project) counts its draft, written feedback and revision days, and every
21:   unit's last day is its end-of-unit assessment. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (no unit here
22:   runs over 25 days, so none has a mid-unit assessment). These are course days: docs/courses/CALENDAR.md places
23:   them on school days. The national figure is higher: the 2007 National Health Education Standards (second
24:   edition, page 76) recommend at least 80 hours a year in grades 3 to 12; this course gives 36 hours a year. The
25:   course page names that figure and the gap so a school can add time (APPROACH.md, Pacing). A sensitive unit's days
26:   count as optional (STANDARD.md section 5 rule 6): a school that leaves one out uses its days to extend the
27:   non-sensitive unit named in its row. A school that runs health as a daily nine-week quarter in each grade keeps
28:   the same order and checks; the scope and sequence says so in its short path.
29: - **Standard.** National Health Education Standards, 2024, SHAPE America: the 46 indicators of the 6-8 span
30:   (`NHES.S<n>.8.<n>`), each owned by one row (13 in grade 6, 17 in grade 7, 16 in grade 8) and placed in one unit
31:   here; ids and our own words only (data/standards/nhes-2024.txt), never the indicator text. Review tool: CDC's
32:   Health Education Curriculum Analysis Tool (HECAT), 2021 edition, keyed to the 2007 standards
33:   (docs/standards-notes/health-2007-to-2024.md); each unit names the healthy behavior outcomes it serves
34:   (`HECAT.<topic>.HBO<n>`), and every day ties to one (bar criterion 1). Every year touches all eight standards in
35:   its non-sensitive units, and every skill standard (2 to 8) is practiced in at least two topics in the band
36:   (STANDARD.md section 4), counted with the sensitive rows taken out so the claim holds in a school that leaves them
37:   out: standard 2 in personal health, food, physical activity, mental health and tobacco; 3 in personal health,
38:   safety and food; 4 in mental health, safety, alcohol and drugs, and violence prevention; 5 in food, tobacco,
39:   safety, physical activity, and alcohol and drugs; 6 in personal health (units 8, 17, 26) and physical activity
40:   (unit 8's activity goal, g6-pa-habits-supports-barriers); 7 in personal health and physical activity; 8 in
41:   safety, tobacco, food and physical activity. The C3 column of the Units table holds, for health, the skill
42:   standard the unit teaches or practices and its step card (the model). Check set: each unit planner reads the
43:   HECAT 2021 module of its topic, 6-8 knowledge and skill expectations, before planning (STANDARD.md section 3); the
44:   "Check set adds" column names what that read is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds,
45:   with the health lines of "What a health day never does" (APPROACH.md). Coverage map: the scope and sequence
46:   carries the 6 to 8 column of STANDARD.md section 4 with unit names in its cells; physical activity outcomes 3, 4
47:   and 6 are taught by moving in PE (Desk q-prework-health-pe-1), and this course names the PE rows instead.
48: - **Base text.** None. Health is Curio Class's own: scenarios, step cards, case cards, invented posts and ads,
49:   community profiles, source cards and family notes are written by Curio Class. Facts and figures come from public
50:   health agencies and national surveys (CDC, the national highway traffic safety agency, the national drug abuse
51:   institute, USDA's food guide pages, the national youth tobacco survey and a national survey of grade 8 students'
52:   alcohol and drug use), each with its source and year, checked by the health expert agent (below). No trade book,
53:   paid video or library visit.
54: - **The health unit shape.** Each unit names its behavior outcomes, three objectives (knowing, valuing, doing) and
55:   one skill with its steps (APPROACH.md). A skill is taught in the bar's five steps: why it matters, the steps, a
56:   model (the teacher says each step aloud on a scenario), practice on a realistic scenario, feedback. The teacher
57:   holds the model on the first fluency day, pairs hold the card on the second, the card is withdrawn on the first
58:   application day, and the end-of-unit assessment is taken without it. At three lessons every two weeks a skill
59:   that returns from an earlier unit or grade gets its card back for one day, then goes without. Norms and pressure
60:   (bar criterion 4): each year states one norm with a current national survey figure for the age group, on an
61:   Understanding day of a non-sensitive unit (APPROACH.md, Kinds of day): grade 6 unit 4, the share of middle school
62:   students who do not use e-cigarettes (the national youth tobacco survey, latest edition); grade 7 unit 12, the
63:   share of grade 8 students who have not had alcohol in the past month (the national survey of grade 8 students,
64:   latest edition, named for its age group); grade 8 unit 25, the same survey's grade 8 figures for alcohol and
65:   cannabis, set beside what students guess first. Each figure is entered with its source and year at the expert
66:   read, never from memory. Pressure is met in the influence units (4, 16, 23), the refusal units (12, 25) and the
67:   dare and group scenes (4, 12).
68: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day"): Understanding (first teaching of a fact or idea, ending in a
69:   choice and a reason), Fluency (practice only: the scripted pair rehearsals of a step card, a refusal line, an
70:   I-statement, a boundary line, a help request), Application (the skill on a new scenario, the student's own goal
71:   or a real audience; drafting and revising an advocacy piece are Application), Check (the end-of-unit assessment)
72:   and Respond (the course-level respond days). Fluency in this course is short: a ten-minute pair rehearsal inside
73:   most days, and at most one whole Fluency day a unit.
74: - **End-of-unit evidence.** Every unit ends on a check day: a sitting with two or three unseen scenarios, read alone
75:   (a short scenario at most, APPROACH.md Supports) or read aloud on request, where each student writes what the
76:   character would say or do using the skill steps, scored on the steps with a guide, plus one facts-in-use item per
77:   fact the unit lists and one valuing item (what would be a good choice for this person and why, scored on whether
78:   the reason uses a unit fact, never on which choice; APPROACH.md). Units that end in a product (18, 26, 27) score
79:   the product on the steps after a counted draft day, written feedback and a revision day; no score comes from a day
80:   a topic is first taught. Twice a unit the student rates their own use of the steps and names one next step (bar
81:   criterion 8). The teacher marks met, partly or not yet. Where an owned indicator asks the student to do something
82:   (NHES.S7.8.3 and NHES.S6.8.5 in unit 26, NHES.S8.8.4 in unit 27), the evidence includes the student doing it at
83:   school: a teacher record of each student carrying out the team's habit and keeping its log, or delivering the
84:   advocacy piece to its audience, scored on the steps and the student's account, never on health, home habits or
85:   whether a goal worked. A character's log or plan shows understanding only and never stands alone for a doing
86:   indicator. Personal plans and self-checks stay with the student unless they choose to share.
87: - **Readiness and retrieval.** The count starts from the grade of the year being taught; there are no routes. Every
88:   row grade 6 assumes is a grade 5 row of Health, grades 3 to 5, taught the year before, except two grade 4 rows;
89:   every row grade 7 assumes is a grade 6 row of this course; grade 8 assumes grade 7 rows, one grade 6 row and one
90:   grade 5 row. A row used the year before opens as a short retrieval task inside a day of the unit that needs it
91:   (sequence-write.md rule 2), named in the unit's row below. Four rows last used two or more grades back get a
92:   readiness check of ten minutes, given before the unit opens: g4-meh-calm-stress (grade 4, Health, grades 3 to 5)
93:   in the last ten minutes of unit 2's last day, before unit 3; g4-phw-prevent-illness-injury (grade 4, Health,
94:   grades 3 to 5) in the last ten minutes of unit 4's last day, before unit 5; g5-phw-surroundings-community (grade 5,
95:   Health, grades 3 to 5) and g6-phw-adult-or-professional (grade 6, unit 2 of this course) in the last ten minutes of
96:   unit 22's day 1, after the hook, since unit 22 opens the grade 8 year. The scope gate's count governs, and the
97:   scope fix pass updates this bullet.
98: - **Sensitive units and the leave-out rule.** STANDARD.md section 5: sensitive content lives only in its own units
99:   (unwanted touch and sexual harassment: unit 9; dating violence and sexual harassment: units 20, 28; self-harm and
100:   suicide: units 19, 29; sexual health: units 10, 21, 30); no skill lives only in a sensitive unit (each indicator a
101:   sensitive row touches is owned by a non-sensitive row of the band: S3.8.1 in unit 2, S4.8.1 in unit 3, S1.8.2 in
102:   unit 1, S4.8.3 in unit 11, S4.8.4 in unit 14, S4.8.7 in unit 26, S4.8.8, S4.8.9 and S4.8.10 in unit 24); getting
103:   help is taught in a non-sensitive unit every year (units 2, 11, 24, with unit 22's care services); no non-sensitive
104:   unit assumes a sensitive one; each sensitive unit has a leave-out note on its first page and a longer family note
105:   (English and Spanish) sent home two weeks before. Each year's sensitive units sit after its non-sensitive units
106:   and right before the year-end check, and no course-level check samples a sensitive unit, so leaving one out
107:   changes no check. In sensitive units rehearsal uses written scripts of the refusal, boundary or telling line only;
108:   the teacher or a card plays any pressure; no student plays a person who pressures, touches, harms or threatens
109:   (APPROACH.md). The self-harm and suicide units (19, 29) follow safe-messaging rules: no method detail, a help line
110:   (the 988 crisis line, named by purpose) and an adult plan on the first page, and a school counselor told ahead
111:   and on call for that lesson.
112: - **Reading level.** Students read at most a short scenario, case card or source card alone; longer text is read
113:   aloud or summarized on the card (APPROACH.md, Supports). Each unit teaches six to ten health words in depth with a
114:   sentence frame for each skill step ("I won't do that. I'm going to ..."; "I feel ___ when ___ because ___. Please ___."). The
115:   right idea in another language, by pointing or with a frame is a language gap, not a content gap. No student must
116:   speak aloud: every rehearsal and check item can be done in writing, by pointing at a card, or with an adult.
117: - **Family notes.** Every unit sends a home task (a talk with a trusted adult the student chooses; a school adult
118:   counts), written in English and Spanish when the unit is finished. No task asks a student about their own drug
119:   use, sexual activity, diet, weight, mental health, abuse or family; scenarios are about characters, and personal
120:   goals stay with the student.
121: - **Devices.** None needed. No owned code needs technology: the source-check and false-message units (13, 23) use
122:   printed source cards and invented posts, and a teacher may show one agency page on one screen. Any Curio Class
123:   practice tool a unit plan adds (a scenario sort, a label reader, a source-check practice) runs on one teacher
124:   screen or a shared device, collects no health information and has a paper path with the same items, so a
125:   paper-path student meets every owned code in full.
126: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper cards, case cards, logs and printed sources; any electronic tool has the
127:   same task on paper beside it, and no student account is ever made.
128: - **Safety.** Hands-on days and their controls are named in the last column of the Units table. Guides the safety
129:   read uses: CDC, "Clean Hands" handwashing pages (https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/); the national highway traffic
130:   safety agency's bicycle safety pages (https://www.nhtsa.gov/road-safety/bicycle-safety) and its impaired driving
131:   pages (https://www.nhtsa.gov/risky-driving/drunk-driving); the school's own active-play, field and duty-of-care
132:   rules for the unit 26 walking group. Links named from memory, opened at the safety and expert reads. No real
133:   medicine, supplement, alcohol, cigarette, vape or lighter is ever brought to class; pictures and invented labels
134:   only. No food is tasted in this course.
135: - **Expert read (bar criterion 7).** The Curio Class health expert agent reads every unit plan and day for medical
136:   and scientific accuracy against current public health sources and records the date (STANDARD.md section 6),
137:   above all the survey figures, the sleep hours by age, the vaccine and infection facts, and the sexual health
138:   units. The course page may say figures were last checked against their sources on a date, and that a school's
139:   health staff should read it too.
140: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
141:   no outside reader is planned (settled 2026-11-14), and the safety and answer-key reads are agent reads too. Units
142:   that need the agent to study named sources first: units 9, 20 and 28 (unwanted touch, harassment, dating violence
143:   and assault: students who have been harmed and their families; the federal Child Welfare Information Gateway
144:   pages on preventing child sexual abuse, starting from https://www.childwelfare.gov/topics/preventing/, CDC's teen
145:   dating violence pages and a national child traumatic stress network's guidance for teachers); units 10, 21 and 30
146:   (sexual health: families of every structure, faith and culture, students of every gender identity and sexual
147:   orientation, students whose bodies change early or differently; the HECAT 2021 Sexual Health module 6-8
148:   expectations and CDC's adolescent sexual health, HIV and infection pages); units 19 and 29 (self-harm and suicide:
149:   students who live with these thoughts, who self-harm, or who have lost someone to suicide; a national suicide
150:   prevention resource center's safe-messaging guidance for schools and the 988 crisis line's pages); units 4, 5, 12,
151:   23 and 25 (tobacco, alcohol, drugs and impaired driving: students who live with someone who uses, families who
152:   have lost someone in a crash; CDC's youth tobacco pages, the national drug abuse institute's pages for teens and
153:   the national highway traffic safety agency's impaired driving pages); units 6 and 16 (food: students with too
154:   little food at home, cultural foods, and the risk of eating-disorder talk; USDA's food guide pages and a national
155:   eating disorders association's guidance for schools); unit 15 (health conditions: students living with asthma,
156:   diabetes, a disability or a long illness; CDC's pages on managing chronic conditions at school); unit 7
157:   (bullying: students who have been bullied or who bully; the federal stop-bullying pages); units 8, 17 and 26
158:   (goals and habits: bodies and weight; the same eating disorders guidance); unit 22 (care: families without
159:   insurance, transport or a regular doctor). Every unit with hands-on work (12, 26) also gets the safety agent's
160:   read against the guides above, and every check gets the answer-key agent's read.
161: - **Course-level checks.** Placed off the core check days (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, grades 6 to 8 by school day,
162:   week 32 a finals week): grade 6's core checks fall in weeks 12, 15, 16, 18, 22 to 24 and 32, grade 7's in weeks
163:   9, 12 to 14, 22, 24 and 32, grade 8's in weeks 10, 14, 15, 18, 20, 24 to 26, 29, 30 and 32. Each year's mid-year
164:   check is course day 25, school day 84 in week 17 (no core check that week in any of the three grades), with its
165:   respond day in week 18; each year-end check is course day 47, school day 157 in week 32, the finals week, with its
166:   respond day on school day 160.
