| 6 | 6 | Dances in many styles. *How do dances from different places use counts and phrases, and how does expressing myself change how much I join in?* | 14 | g6-dance-sequences; g6-express-variety | SHAPE.S1.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.1; SHAPE.S4.8.3 | Cues: "count, level, transition". Settings: a line dance, a partner social dance, a group's own rhythmic routine | Movement sequences in a range of dance styles: eight-count phrases, transitions and a sequence of four phrases (S1.8.2). Picks: cumbia's basic step, a social partner dance from Colombia's Caribbean coast, taught as its basic side and back steps in eight counts with teacher-set partners, no gender roles and a hand hold or side by side; a four-wall line dance in eight counts of our own, with no cultural claim; and a group's own rhythmic routine. Explain how self-expression affects how involved a person gets (S4.8.1), and take part in many kinds of activity that improve health, recording the kinds taken part in this year (units 2 and 3 fitness, units 4 and 5 team games, this unit's dances) and saying which fits best (S4.8.3, in full); goal-cycle and calm-steps first used here (a goal for the routine; a calm step before performing for a small group). Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's gym and dance sequence and review of experiences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a four-phrase sequence to the beat, the baseline cue "transition"; knowledge task: one way self-expression changed how a person joined in, and the kind of activity that fits best and why; conduct: welcomes a partner's ideas in the group routine, S3.8.1 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or the teacher's count; dance cards and each dance's source line (Curio Class writes them) | Dance needs a year of movement ideas (unit 5) for levels and transitions; S4 sits here because a performance is where self-expression shows, and the record looks back on the year's kinds of activity | Candidate: groups teach each other one phrase | Cultural content: Colombian cumbia, a living social dance; taught with respect, no costume or imitation of a people; sacred or ceremonial dances never used (Cory, 2026-11-20). Not opened at a page in the fix pass (named from memory): cumbia is a candidate until the agent confirms what it is, its roots and its basic step at a Colombian cultural institution's page (For the lead 5). It replaced tinikling (dance K to 2, grade 2) and the Virginia Reel (PE 3 to 5, grade 3), so no pick repeats an earlier grade. Performing is for a small group, never alone before the class unless the student chooses. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
| 7 | 6 | Strike, serve and stay active. *Which striking and net activities could I keep doing, and where could I find ways to stay active?* | 14 | g6-strike-net-toward; g6-ways-active | SHAPE.S2.8.16 | Cues: "ready, turn, swing, recover"; "toss, step, contact". Settings: a handheld forehand to a wall, a swing at a tossed ball off a cone, an underhand serve with a short tool over a low rope net; a sampler of lifetime activities | Work toward the striking and net skills grade 7 owns: a handheld forehand, a swing at a tossed ball and an underhand serve with a short tool, in modified rallies over a low net with light equipment, each begun toward its owner: SHAPE.S1.8.5 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.7 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.10 and SHAPE.S1.8.11 (grade 7, unit 12), SHAPE.S1.8.12 (grade 7, unit 12). List ways to stay active (team sports, individual sports, dance, outdoor, community programs, active transport), with ones that fit different interests, budgets and abilities, and how to find each (S2.8.16). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's net and wall and striking skills and goals, and a readiness check on g4-reasons-strengths (reasons to move) at the end of unit 6. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a swing, the baseline cue "eyes on contact"; knowledge task: a personal list of five ways to stay active with how to find each; conduct: one swinger per space and courtesy in the rally, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: short paddles, foam bats, light balls, a rope net; community activity cards (invented for practice, no program or company names) | Last in grade 6: the striking tries hand straight into grade 7's striking units, and ways to stay active closes the year looking outward; its unit assessment gives its evidence, and the year-end check carries that record unscored | Candidate: a family "active in our area" card sent with the family note | Swing space cleared on every side; one swinger per space; stand beside, never in front. Activity lists respect cost and access: free options first; no student is asked what their family can afford. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $125 (15 paddles, 8 foam bats, light balls, a rope net) |
| 11 | 7 | Swing, place and play fair. *How do I swing a long tool with a full pattern and place my hit, and how do we settle disagreements in a game we officiate ourselves?* | 12 | g7-long-tool-swing; g7-strike-tossed; g7-settle-disagreements | SHAPE.S1.8.5; SHAPE.S1.8.7; SHAPE.S3.8.8; SHAPE.S3.8.9 | Cues: "grip, side stance, shift, rotate, contact, follow through". Settings: a tee, a soft toss, a self-drop; zone hitting drills; a small self-officiated hitting and fielding game with no outs that remove a player | Swing a long-handled tool to hit with a mature pattern, with a bat, a hockey-style stick or a golf-style club on a tee or a tossed ball (S1.8.5); strike a self-dropped or tossed ball with a tool into open space, choosing a place to hit (S1.8.7); settle disagreements with teammates and with opponents using calm steps and team talk (S3.8.8) and use and respect the norms of good conduct: honest calls, a handshake or fist bump (S3.8.9). Fielding the hit keeps g6-catch-form in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first swings and grade 6's rules, and a readiness check on g5-conflict-solve at the end of unit 10. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a mature pattern, three of five, the baseline cue "rotate"; a hit placed to a named zone as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: the steps to settle a pictured disagreement; conduct: settles a real call and keeps the norms in the self-officiated game), 1 respond day. Tools: foam bats, tees (cones), soft balls | After unit 7's first swings and unit 8's team plans; settling disagreements fits a self-officiated game, the first of the year | Candidate: the class writes its own three good-conduct norms | Swing space cleared; one swinger per space; stand beside, never in front; foam balls. No role play of a conflict between students; the disagreement cards use invented players. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $20 (4 more foam bats) |
| 12 | 7 | Serve, stroke and keep your cool. *How do I serve and rally over a net, and what do I do when the game gets frustrating?* | 14 | g7-serves; g7-strokes; g7-manage-feelings | SHAPE.S1.8.10; SHAPE.S1.8.11; SHAPE.S1.8.12; SHAPE.S4.8.8 | Cues: "toss, step, contact, follow through"; "ready, turn, swing, recover"; SEL's "stop, breathe, name it, pick a tool, go on". Settings: serves to a wall and over a low net, alone and with a partner, a modified net game with a choice of a more or less competitive court | Serve with the hand, underhand and overhand (S1.8.10, in full), and with a short or long tool, underhand and overhand (S1.8.11, in full), in drills and modified net and wall games; forehand and backhand strokes with a short or long tool (S1.8.12, in full: both strokes, both tool lengths across the stations); use several strategies to manage feelings and behavior during activity: slow breathing, self-talk, a short reset, asking for a role change (S4.8.8; calm-steps in full, in SEL's grade 6 words). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first serves and strokes and grade 6's self-expression, and a readiness check on g5-review-experiences in the last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: serves into the court to a named zone, the baseline cue "toss, step": hand underhand and overhand, tool underhand and overhand, each marked apart across the two check days; forehand and backhand strokes with a short and a long tool, each marked apart, as the last Fluency days' closure watches, carried; knowledge task: name three strategies and when to use each; conduct: uses one strategy after a lost point, watched, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: paddles, light balls, beach or trainer balls for hand serves, rope nets | After the mid-year check: serves and strokes need unit 11's swing control; net games bring the most lost points, so managing feelings is taught where frustration is real | Candidate: a reset card the student keeps in a pocket | Emotions: a student may step out for a reset with a role; it is never a punishment; the teacher page names the school counselor; PE does not treat mental health conditions. Sensitivity read sources: the national mental health institute's pages on stress in teens. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $40 (10 trainer balls) |
| 13 | 7 | Change the dance. *How can space, time, force and relationship change a dance, and how do friends and choices keep people joining in?* | 13 | g7-dance-ideas; g7-people-interests; g7-challenges | SHAPE.S2.8.8; SHAPE.S4.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.4; SHAPE.S4.8.5 | Cues: "same steps, new idea: unison, canon, contrast". Settings: grade 6's line dance and a circle dance changed by groups, a rhythm form with sticks or claps | Use movement ideas (space, time, force, relationship) to change different dance and rhythm forms: a unison section, a canon, a contrast, in a line dance, a circle dance and a rhythm form (S2.8.8). Pick: kolo, a Serbian chain dance danced in a circle at community gatherings (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2017), taught in a simple step pattern, then changed with canon and contrast. Explain how time with others affects involvement (S4.8.2) and link choice and interests to how engaged a person becomes (S4.8.4): groups choose their dance style and roles; closely examine challenges faced alone and as a group in movement (fear of failing, space, a fitness gap) and test one fix in rehearsal (S4.8.5). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 6's sequences and self-expression and unit 5's movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: perform a sequence changed with one idea, the baseline cue "canon"; knowledge tasks: say which idea changed it; say how working with others changed an invented dancer's joining in; connect a choice to staying involved; name a challenge with a fix and say how the fix went; conduct: includes every group member's idea, S3.8.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or claps; rhythm sticks (rolled paper on the low-equipment path) | Dance returns each year (units 6, 13, 18, 22) so it is never taught once and dropped; involvement and challenges sit with group choreography, where choice and others shape joining in | Candidate: a canon the class performs in two halves | Cultural content: Serbian community dance tradition; taught with respect, no costume; no sacred use. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: UNESCO's Representative List entry "Kolo, traditional folk dance" (https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/kolo-traditional-folk-dance-01270) describes a social chain dance that involves the whole community; the Serbian national heritage register's entry on kolo (https://nkns.rs/cyr/popis-nkns/kolo-kolo-u-tri-kolo-u-shest, opened by the second-model read 2026-11-25) gives its context, and the agent confirms the simple step pattern at it first; the students' canon and contrast are their own choreography, not the tradition. Challenges: fear of failing named kindly; no student's challenge is shared without consent. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
