ok back on the year's training (unit 10) | Candidate: a private "how I recovered" log for one week | Food: no body weight, calorie counts or diet talk; foods a class commonly sees; allergies respected; rest is never a punishment or a reward. Outdoors: weather, route, sun, water and adult ratio checked first; stay in groups; a buddy rule. Sensitivity read sources: the federal dietary guidelines' adolescent pages and the national sleep guidance for teens on a federal health page. Safety read (outdoor). Cost: $5 (printing) |
| 15 | 8 | My test, my goal, my tools. *How can I choose a fitness test, set a goal and track it privately, and which tools would really help?* | 12 | g8-fitness-test-track; g8-tech-judge; g8-goals-obstacles | SHAPE.S2.8.10; SHAPE.S2.8.19; SHAPE.S4.8.6; SHAPE.S4.8.7 | Cues: "baseline, goal, plan, retest". Settings: test stations by choice (a walk or run at a self-set pace for a fixed time, with the distance recorded privately, a push-up count, a sit-and-reach), the student's own record sheet, tool cards | Choose a personal fitness test and use it to track progress toward a goal over a term, privately and by choice: a baseline, a goal, a plan built with unit 10's principles, a retest and a plan change if progress stalls, with the time, route and recording kept the same at baseline and retest (S2.8.10, in full); judge how useful technology is for reaching activity and fitness goals: accuracy, privacy, cost and what a tool cannot tell, on printed tool cards invented for practice (S2.8.19); set participation goals from an honest look at one's own abilities (S4.8.6) and weigh the chances and obstacles for being active outside class: time, cost, transport, weather (S4.8.7). The retest uses a plot of the student's own results over weeks on a supplied grid with labeled week and result axes, leaning on math grade 6 unit 6 (6.EE.C.9, days 96 to 117) two years before. Days: 10 in weeks 1 to 4 (course days 1 to 10), then the held check day (the retest and plan change, with the unit's end-of-unit knowledge task) on course day 47 (week 16, after unit 18) and its respond day on day 48, so the plan runs a term in every warm-up between; the scope and sequence sets them in days.csv. Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 10's training principles and effort and unit 13's challenges. Ends: 1 check day (the held day; skill task: the chosen test done with form, the baseline cue "steady pace"; knowledge tasks: shows the teacher the plan change and its reason, not the numbers; rates a tool card on three points; a participation goal, an obstacle and a plan around it; conduct: keeps others' results private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day (day 48) | First in grade 8 so a goal can be tracked over a term; it builds on grade 7's training principles and challenges, and its goal and obstacle rows give the year its S4 thread | Candidate: a private plot of the student's own test, never collected | Fitness test results are the student's own: never posted, graded or compared; any student may choose a different test or a role; no BMI, weight or body measures. No tool is required and no personal data is collected by the course. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' youth chapter and the professional body's position on fitness testing (the agent finds it on SHAPE America's free pages). Safety read (fitness testing). Cost: $5 (a ruler on a box for the sit-and-reach) |
| 18 | 8 | My planned week, and leading a warm-up. *How do I plan a week of routine and circuit with good form, and what makes a good leader?* | 8 | g8-fitness-dance-apply; g8-leading | SHAPE.S3.8.10 | Cues: "posture, tempo, transition"; "clear, kind, on time". Settings: a self-chosen routine or circuit, a small-group warm-up led by one student | Dance routines and fitness circuits used to show form in a planned week, with a self-chosen routine: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.8.2 (grade 6, unit 6), SHAPE.S1.8.3 (grade 6, unit 3) and SHAPE.S1.8.4 (grade 6, unit 2). Describe how communication, feedback, teamwork and courtesy connect to leading, and lead a small-group warm-up or drill (S3.8.10). Unit 15's held check and respond days (course days 47 and 48) follow this unit, so units 16 and 17 start two days earlier than the even run: unit 16 days 11 to 25, unit 17 days 26 to 38, this unit's 8 days 39 to 46; the calendar file shows the even run (unit 15 on days 1 to 12), and the scope sets the exact days. Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 14's circuit and routine and unit 8's feedback and unit 11's conduct norms. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: perform the routine or circuit with correct form, the baseline cue "posture"; knowledge task: link each of communication, feedback, teamwork and courtesy to a pictured leader's choice; conduct: leads a three-minute warm-up for a small group), 1 respond day | Just before the mid-year check, which carries its record; the leadership, the year's S3 owned code, is sampled at the year-end check; short because its motor row reuses owned grade 6 codes | Candidate: a warm-up card a student leads for another class group | Leading is for a small group, never the whole class unless the student chooses. Music optional. Cost: $0 |
